    "question": "What should compile-time tests verify?",
    "quickAnswer": "Compile-time tests should verify important public type relationships and expected failures, not the compiler's own implementation details. They protect contracts that runtime tests cannot observe.",
    "conceptualExplanation": "A type-test file uses assignability assertions or expected-error directives alongside runtime tests for the corresponding behavior.",
    "productionTradeOff": "Overly detailed type tests are brittle and can obstruct safe refactoring, so test only relationships that consumers intentionally depend on.",
